---
// D445 — bespoke in-kernel reconstruction.
//
// The page IS the recreation: a book page, not a screen. A drawn border frames
// the viewport, a single justified column of running text holds the centre with
// footnote markers threaded through it, and the notes themselves hang on a short
// rule at the foot of the frame — the printed page's own apparatus. The one live
// mechanic is the reading control: image-zoom is OFF by default (the signature
// state — a page you read, not a gallery you pinch), and a lone toggle lets a
// curious reader switch zoom ON, at which point the specimen plate becomes a
// lens. De-branded: the source's plate is a kernel specimen, not the client's
// artwork, and the only spot of colour marks where that artwork sat.
//
// No reference render exists for this work (refs/works/d445.jpg was never
// captured), so — unlike the measured pages in this set — the geometry here is
// NOT transcribed from a source at 1600px. It is composed from reading
// typography's own constants instead: a measure in the 60–72ch band, footnotes a
// step down on a short rule, a double-ruled frame inset from the edge. Honest
// first principles, not invented pixels.
//
// Archived case-study copy (removed from DOM 2026-07-17):
//
//   The signature —
//   Everything here defers to reading. The page draws its own border so the text
//   sits inside an object, not a window; the column takes exactly one comfortable
//   measure and no more; and the footnotes stay where footnotes belong, on a
//   short rule at the foot, addressed by number rather than by hover. Zoom is
//   withheld by default because zoom is a way of not reading — the control exists,
//   but the page opens in the state that trusts the reader.
//
//   Study notes —
//   A reading page is mostly restraint: the measure is the design decision, and
//   once it is set the margins, the footnote size, and the frame all fall out of
//   it. The mechanic had to be a toggle, not a hover, because a hover-zoom would
//   fire every time the eye crossed the plate on its way down the column — the
//   opposite of reading. So the default is off, the switch is explicit, and the
//   lens only arms once the reader asks for it.
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('works', 'd445');
const w = entry!.data;
---

<Base title={`${w.title} — *blue red + purple/`} chrome="work">
  <main class="site">
    <div class="page">
      <article class="reader">
        <header class="reader__head">
          <p class="reader__folio font-mono">{w.title} · fol. 1</p>
          <h1 class="reader__title">A Page, Read Rather Than Pinched</h1>
        </header>

        <div class="reader__col">
          <p>
            A page is a room entered through the left margin, and this one draws its own walls so
            the text sits inside an object rather than floating in a window.<sup
              ><a id="ref1" href="#fn1">1</a></sup
            > The measure is the first and only real decision; once the line length is set to a
            comfortable span, the inner margins, the footnote size, and the frame all fall out of it
            without further argument.
          </p>

          <figure class="plate" aria-describedby="plate-cap">
            <span class="plate__mark font-mono">fig. i</span>
            <figcaption id="plate-cap" class="plate__cap font-mono">specimen · zoom disabled</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Zoom is withheld here on purpose, because zoom is a way of not reading — a hover that
            magnifies the plate would fire every time the eye crossed it on the way down the
            column.<sup><a id="ref2" href="#fn2">2</a></sup> The control still exists, bottom-right, but
            the page opens in the state that trusts the reader, and the lens only arms once the reader
            explicitly asks for it. Until then the specimen is simply an illustration, sized to the
            measure and left alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <footer class="reader__notes">
          <ol class="reader__fn">
            <li id="fn1">
              The border is double-ruled — an outer line with an inset companion — the oldest way to
              say “this is a page, and it ends here.” <a href="#ref1" aria-label="Back to reference 1">↩</a>
            </li>
            <li id="fn2">
              Reading mode = zoom off. Toggle it on and the plate becomes a lens; click the plate to
              magnify, click again to release. <a href="#ref2" aria-label="Back to reference 2">↩</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </footer>

        <button class="reader__toggle font-mono" type="button" aria-pressed="false">
          Zoom · Off
        </button>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  // The one live mechanic: reading mode defaults to zoom OFF. The toggle flips a
  // data-zoom flag on the reader; only while it is set does the plate arm as a
  // lens, and only a click (never a hover) magnifies it — a hover would fire on
  // every pass of the eye, which is the behaviour the default exists to prevent.
  const reader = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.reader');
  const toggle = reader?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.reader__toggle');
  const plate = reader?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.plate');

  toggle?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const on = reader!.toggleAttribute('data-zoom');
    toggle.textContent = on ? 'Zoom · On' : 'Zoom · Off';
    toggle.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
    if (!on) plate?.removeAttribute('data-lens'); // leaving reading mode releases the lens
  });

  plate?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    if (reader?.hasAttribute('data-zoom')) plate.toggleAttribute('data-lens');
  });
</script>

<style>
  /* The rebuilt room, now the whole page. One viewport tall, the frame inset from
     every edge, and the column cropped at the fold if it runs long — the page is
     an object with a bottom, not an endless scroll.

     The page is its own query container: cq* units below resolve against the
     viewport width, since no stage wraps a work page any more. */
  .site {
    container-type: inline-size;
    display: grid;
    place-items: stretch;
    min-height: 100dvh;
    max-height: 100dvh;
    padding: clamp(1rem, 3.2cqw, 3.5rem);
    background-color: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* The drawn page border: an outer rule plus an inset companion rule (::after),
     the printed page's oldest frame. Not a box around content — the object the
     text lives inside. */
  .page {
    position: relative;
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    padding: clamp(1.4rem, 3cqw, 3.2rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .page::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: clamp(6px, 0.8cqw, 12px);
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .reader {
    display: contents;
  }

  /* Masthead: a mono folio line above a single display title, both hung on the
     column's own left edge (the measure is centred by margin below). */
  .reader__head {
    width: min(64ch, 100%);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .reader__folio {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }
  .reader__title {
    margin: 0.5cqw 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--wght-display, 700);
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4cqw, 3.4rem);
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display, -0.02em);
  }

  /* The single measure: the whole design decision. ~62ch, centred, justified the
     way set type is, hyphenation on so the ragged edge does not fight the frame. */
  .reader__col {
    width: min(62ch, 100%);
    margin: clamp(1.2rem, 3.4cqw, 3rem) auto 0;
    align-self: start;
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.35cqw, 1.4rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
    text-align: justify;
    text-wrap: pretty;
    hyphens: auto;
  }
  .reader__col p {
    margin: 0;
    text-indent: 1.6em;
  }
  .reader__col p:first-child {
    text-indent: 0; /* opening paragraph sets flush, as set text does */
  }
  .reader__col sup a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-feature-settings: 'sups';
  }
  .reader__col sup a:hover,
  .reader__col sup a:focus-visible {
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }

  /* The specimen. Inert while reading (default cursor, no scale). When zoom is
     armed it becomes a lens: cursor invites the click, and a lensed click scales
     it in place above the text. The spot ink marks where the artwork sat. */
  .plate {
    position: relative;
    width: min(22ch, 70%);
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
    margin: clamp(1rem, 2.4cqw, 2rem) auto;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    gap: 0.4em;
    background-color: var(--paper-1, var(--paper));
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    transition: scale var(--dur, 0.3s) var(--ease, ease);
    transform-origin: center;
    z-index: 0;
  }
  .plate__mark {
    font-size: clamp(0.8rem, 1.6cqw, 1.4rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }
  .plate__cap {
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }
  .reader[data-zoom] .plate {
    cursor: zoom-in;
  }
  .reader[data-zoom] .plate[data-lens] {
    scale: 1.8;
    cursor: zoom-out;
    z-index: 3;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow, 0 1cqw 3cqw rgb(0 0 0 / 0.18));
  }

  /* Footnotes: a step down, hung on a short rule at the foot of the frame — where
     footnotes belong. The rule is short and left-set, the printed convention. */
  .reader__notes {
    width: min(62ch, 100%);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-top: clamp(0.8rem, 1.6cqw, 1.4rem);
  }
  .reader__notes::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 8ch;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    margin-bottom: clamp(0.5rem, 1cqw, 0.9rem);
  }
  .reader__fn {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.6em;
    font-size: clamp(0.78rem, 1cqw, 1rem);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }
  .reader__fn li + li {
    margin-top: 0.4em;
  }
  .reader__fn a {
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .reader__fn a:hover,
  .reader__fn a:focus-visible {
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }

  /* The reading control: the page's only interactive chrome, tucked bottom-right
     inside the frame, clear of the ← INDEX mark. Reads as a marginal switch, not
     a button-button. */
  .reader__toggle {
    position: absolute;
    right: clamp(1.4rem, 3cqw, 3.2rem);
    bottom: clamp(1.4rem, 3cqw, 3.2rem);
    padding: 0.4em 0.7em;
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color var(--dur, 0.3s) var(--ease, ease);
    z-index: 4;
  }
  .reader__toggle:hover,
  .reader__toggle:focus-visible {
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
    outline: none;
  }
  .reader[data-zoom] .reader__toggle {
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }

  /* Motion is a nicety, not the mechanic — reduced-motion keeps the lens instant. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .plate,
    .reader__toggle {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* Phone: the source page adapting, not a second design. A reading column is
     already single-measure, so it just narrows to the device; the frame's inset
     tightens and the type rides its rem floors. */
  @container (max-width: 40rem) {
    .reader__col,
    .reader__head,
    .reader__notes {
      width: 100%;
    }
    .reader__col {
      text-align: left;
      hyphens: manual;
    }
    .plate {
      width: min(24ch, 80%);
    }
  }
</style>
